# Allow-list, not a deny-list. `COPY . .` (Dockerfile:8) otherwise ships whatever
# happens to sit in the checkout, and the worst residents are rewritten on a timer:
# config/private.yaml hourly by the tg-sub-crawler container (docker-compose.yaml:38),
# config/.geoblock.db-wal continuously, config/.crawler-state.json and
# config/.stable-snapshot.json once per cycle. A deny-list that misses one of those
# silently re-invalidates the COPY layer and every layer below it, which is the whole
# bug this file exists to fix. Inverting it makes the context exactly the compile
# inputs, so anything added to the tree later stays out until someone opts it in.
#
# Nothing else is needed to build: there is no //go:embed anywhere in the tree, and the
# runtime stage (Dockerfile:12-16) copies only the compiled binary out of the builder,
# so no config, doc, or deploy file can reach the published image by any route.
#
# Cost of the inversion: adding a //go:embed later fails the compile with
# "pattern ...: no matching files found" until its asset is unignored here. That is a
# loud failure at build time, unlike the silent cache miss a deny-list leaks.
*

# Resolved on their own layer before the source copy (Dockerfile:5-6).
!go.mod
!go.sum

# The main package: main.go, plus the whole internal/ tree, which holds .go files and
# nothing else.
!main.go
#
# `!internal` has to be the bare directory name, and it is not interchangeable with the
# tighter-looking `!internal/**/*.go`. Both context walkers refuse to descend into an
# ignored directory unless some un-ignore pattern's LITERAL text prefix-extends that
# directory's path: `internal/**/*.go` does that for `internal/` but not for
# `internal/app/`, and only BuildKit compensates for the `**`. Measured here (docker
# 29.6.2, buildx 0.35.0, buildkit 0.31.2) with `internal/**/*.go` as the only un-ignore:
# BuildKit shipped 62 files, `DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0` shipped 4 -- internal/ absent outright
# -- and the compile died on "no required module provides package
# domains.lst/sub-preprocessor/internal/app". `docker compose build` honours
# DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0, so that is reachable from the documented deploy path. With the bare
# name both walkers ship the same 62 files, so do not "simplify" this back.
#
# The cost is granularity: un-ignoring a directory un-ignores everything under it, so a
# non-.go file dropped into internal/ rides along -- today there are none. Re-ignoring
# part of the tree later is not blocked, on either walker: appending `internal/app` (or
# `internal/app/`) drops that package identically under both, 60 files / 503,823 bytes.
# The wall above bites only the un-ignore side, and only when a pattern's literal text
# stops prefix-extending the directory: after that re-ignore, `!internal/app/app.go`
# keeps that one file on both walkers (61 / 512,547), while the same intent spelled
# `!internal/**/app.go` keeps it on BuildKit and loses it on the classic one (61 vs 60).
# So a future internal/testdata/ can be excluded -- just spell any exception reaching
# under it out literally, never through `**`.
!internal

# `go build` reads default.pgo from the main package directory on its own (-pgo=auto is
# the default since Go 1.21). Dropping it still produces a working binary, just a
# quietly slower one -- a regression no smoke test would catch.
!default.pgo

# The builder only runs `go build`, so tests never compile here, and they are 60% of
# internal/ by size; excluding them lets a test-only commit reuse the cached compile.
# Gotcha if that changes: `go test ./...` against this context reports "no test files"
# for all 21 packages and PASSES (exit 0), so a builder test step needs BOTH this line
# deleted and `!config/config.yaml` added -- internal/config/config_specs_test.go:532
# and internal/preprocess/processor_internal_test.go:1178 read ../../config/config.yaml
# and t.Fatal on error. Measured: deleting this line alone fails TestShippedConfigLoads,
# TestShippedConfigDropsASNButKeepsTheCapability and TestASNFilterFormsStillBuild with
# "open ../../config/config.yaml: no such file or directory"; with both changes
# `go test ./internal/config/ ./internal/preprocess/` passes. One residue is not this
# file's problem: the builder stage runs as root, so the 0500-directory assertion in
# TestSaveSnapshotFailureKeepsPreviousFile cannot fail as designed and `go test ./...`
# still exits 1 there.
internal/**/*_test.go
